## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: Enzymatic biofuel cells Glucose biosensor Metal-organic frameworks Cellulose acetate Long-term monitoring
Development of reliable Enzymatic Biofuel Cells (EBFC)-based self-powered glucose biosensor for continuous, noninvasive monitoring without restriction on patient’s movement is highly recommendable. However, its application to a large extent is limited by the relatively poor stability. Herein, we synthesized a highly flexible electrode for effective enzyme immobilization by encapsulating enzyme into the metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and robustly anchored to the cellulose acetate (CA) nanofiber membrane. As is well-known, such nanostructured fiber materials are the first time to be synthesized for glucose biosensor, which encapsulated biomolecules in MOFs platform during the MOFs in-situ growth on the nanofiber membranes. The as-proposed biosensor demonstrated excellent stability over $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ of continuous long-term monitoring. The remarkable sta­ bility of assembled self-powered glucose biosensor in this work could inspire the application of enzymatic bio­ sensors in biometrics, chronic disease management and clinical diagnosis.
## 1. Introduction
The glucose level in blood is a critical technical indicator due to glucose as an essential energy source for cellular activity in the living body can closely affect the physiological homeostasis system of the human body (Lee et al., 2018). However, a mass of people suffer from diabetes caused by metabolic disorder involving glucose processing, which is also one of the most extensive chronic diseases caused by the dysfunction of insulin secretion (Gao et al., 2016). More seriously, excess glucose in the blood plasma leads to complications such as blindness, cardiovascular disease and kidney failure (Lipani et al., 2018). Therefore, it is very urgent to monitor and control blood glucose levels (Lin et al., 2019a). Electrochemical analysis becomes an ideal explore by many researchers due to its selectivity (Park et al., 2018), sensitivity (Kwon et al., 2019) and miniaturization (Barton et al., 2004), additionally, electrochemical sensor can transform analytical substrates into electrical signals through oxidation-reduction reactions, which have been achieved for protein (Chen et al., 2019), heavy metal ions (Nyein et al., 2016) and other biomarkers of exploration (Sakthivel et al., 2019). As a novel electrochemical sensor (Xu et al., 2010), EBFC-based self-powered biosensor with advantages of low cost (Wang et al., 2018), anti-interference (Katz et al., 2001) and no need for extra power supply (Miyake et al., 2011), has attracted considerable attention in recent years and has been already successfully utilized in various biomarkers (Fan et al., 2020). Sun. et al. prepared a EBFC-based sensor for formaldehyde detection (Sun et al., 2019a). Lv. et al. reported a self-powered sensor for glucose detection by conductive bacterial cel­ lulose (Lv et al., 2018). We established a self-powered sensing platform for bisphenol A detection (Li et al., 2020a). However, the long-term working stability of the self-powered biosensor is still a challenge due to the core position of the enzyme in the device (Yang and Gao, 2019).
Enzymes are key biomolecules capable of effectively catalyzing lifesustaining biological conversion in complex ecosystems (Chen et al., 2020). Inspired by their outstanding specificity and selectivity (Mah­ moodi and Saffar-Dastgerdi, 2020), increasing efforts are devoted to apply enzymatic transformations in vivo to sensing devices as signal conductors and amplifiers (Li et al., 2020b). However, the inherent fragility of the enzyme restricts the effectiveness of the enzyme system in practical operation (Bornscheuer et al., 2012), such as its vulnerability to surrounding fluctuations (Sheldon and Woodley, 2018) and its low tolerance to most organic solvents (Schmid et al., 2001) and small molecule inhibitors (Klibanov, 2001). Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted extensive attention in separation (Lian et al., 2017), catalysis (Huo et al., 2015), sensing and drug delivery in past few years (Doonan et al., 2017), due to its merits of high surface area and porosity (Pang et al., 2018), diversity and versatile framework compo­ sitions (Liang et al., 2015). MOFs have been used as exoskeletons to protect vulnerable biological macromolecules including enzymes (Hu et al., 2018), DNA, and even viruses in very recent works (Feng et al., 2019). Chen. et al. encapsulated glucose oxidase (GOx) and horse radish peroxidase (HRP) in ZIF-8 NMOFs (Chen et al., 2018). Song et al. encapsulated multiple enzyme and DNA in ZIF-8 crystal (Song et al., 2019). Our research group encapsulated laccase (LAC) into ZIF-8 with unprecedented stability in previous work (Li et al., 2020a). This novel embedding strategy is not only capable of improving the stability of encapsulation enzymes by structural constraints, but also allows the selective transfer via the accessible microporous network (Zhang et al., 2019). Such symbiotic enhancement endows enzymes with great po­ tential in biocatalysis (Zhang et al., 2018), biosensors and drug carrier for cancer (Zhou et al., 2019).
Flexible electronics hold great prospect in biomedical applications, such as artificial muscle, implantable biodevices (Gwon et al., 2014), skin sensors and electronic skin (Yousaf et al., 2016). Flexible electrodes as key link require strong mechanical property (Wen et al., 2016), pre­ cise integration of redox moieties and flexible independence (Li et al., 2014). Nevertheless, integrating the aforesaid merits within a single electrode is still a challenge (Wang et al., 2017). Cellulose/carbon nanotubes (CNTs)-based platforms have been considered ideal flexible electrode materials for lithium batteries (Moghadam et al., 2015), fuel cells (Pan et al., 2016), and sensors in recent years (Yin et al., 2016). Cellulose is a biocompatible linear polysaccharide (Kim et al., 2018), and the characteristics of sustainability, intrinsic flexibility and high strength (Ullah et al., 2016) make cellulose conducive to the long-term stability of the sensor in vitro and in vivo (Kim et al., 2019). Moreover, cellulose can utilize as inherent electrolyte reservoir benefit by hydro­ philicity (Mulyadi et al., 2017). The hierarchical porous structure capable of transferring the diffuse ions in electrolytes and electrolytes to electrochemical active materials (Tedeschi et al., 2018). CNTs have been acted as conductive nanowires to effectively promote direct electron transfer (DET) because of its advantages of high surface area (Zhang and Zhao, 2009), high conductivity (Liu et al., 2014), tunable functional surface and nanoscale size (Choudhary et al., 2017). Xu. et al. designed a foldable electrodes based on nanocellulose and carbon nanotubes for energy storage devices (Xu et al., 2018). Our research group reported a novel single-enzymatic biofuel cell utilizing bacterial cellulose/CNTs-based highly flexible conductive electrode (Li et al., 2020b).
Gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) are noble metal nanoparticles with great potential to be widely used in food safety detection (Daniel and Astruc, 2004), biological analysis and catalysis due to its excellent biocompat­ ibility (Jain et al., 2008), chemical inertness and abundant electronic and optical properties (Hu et al., 2006). Importantly, benefit by its chemical inertness and low toxicity (Jin, 2012), AuNPs was used to connect biomolecules (Oliveira et al., 2009) for biosensors based on DET between active center and electrode (Lim et al., 2017). In this work, we explored the in-situ growth of ZIF-8 on electrospun cellulose acetate (CA) nanofiber membranes with encapsulating LAC and glucose oxidase (GOx) into ZIF-8 during the growth process, respectively. Moreover, the highly flexible conductive electrodes were prepared by adsorbing mul­ tiwalled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) and AuNPs step by step on CA/ZIF-8@enzyme membranes. Furthermore, we established a platform of self-powered biosensor to monitor glucose utilizing nanostructured electrodes of CA/ZIF-8@GOx/MWCNTs/Au and CA/ZIF-8@LAC/ MWCNTs/Au as anode and cathode, respectively. The as-proposed self-powered glucose sensors demonstrated a significant enhancement of long-term stability with continuous work of up to $15\mathrm{h}$ . The strategy of in-situ growth of MOFs on cellulose nanofiber with encapsulating enzyme into MOFs in the meantime, provides an innovative approach to establish various stable and reliable EBFC-based biosensors.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Cellulose acetate (CA, $\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{w}}=840,000{\mathrm{~D}}\mathfrak{s}$ ) was provided by Nantong Cellulose Fibers Co., Ltd. (Nantong, China). LAC from Ganoderma lucidum, GOx from Aspergillus niger and D-glucose were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO). Multiwalled carbon nanotubes (water dispersions, $10~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) were supplied from Nanjing XFNANO Ma­ terials Tech Co., Ltd. (Nanjing, China). Besides, Acetone $\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O C H_{3})}$ , N,N-dimethylacetamide (DMAC), 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM), zinc ni­ trate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC), hydro­ chloroauric acid trihydrate $(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99.9\%$ ) and sodium citrate $(\mathrm{Na_{3}C_{6}H_{5}O_{7}{\cdot}2H_{2}O)}$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd (Shanghai, China) and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of CA nanofiber membrane
CA nanofiber membrane was prepared by electrospinning. $12\mathrm{wt}\%$ of CA spinning solution which used the mixture of acetone and DMAC (volume ratio is 3:2) as solvent was stirring for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . The CA nanofibers were obtained by electrospinning technique through the self-made electrospinning device. The electrospun CA nanofiber membrane was then dried at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for future usage.
## 2.3. Synthesis of CA/ZIF-8@enzyme
Scheme S1 illustrates the synthesis mechanism of CA/ZIF8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au electrode. Specifically, $80~\mathrm{\mL}$ zinc nitrate solution containing $10~\mathrm{mL}$ enzyme was stirred for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, CA nanofiber membrane was immersed in the mixture solution for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After washing by PBS solution, the membrane was transferred to $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of 2-MIM solution without stirring for 1 d. Finally, the obtained CA/ZIF$8@$ enzyme membrane was washed thoroughly to remove the impurities attached to the surface.
## 2.4. Preparation of CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au electrode
The obtained CA/ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ enzyme was firstly soaked in CNTs disper­ sions for a few seconds and then dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Followed through adsorption with AuNPs solution, the CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au was successfully prepared (Fig. S1). AuNPs were synthesized in one step. $\mathrm{\HAuCl}_{4}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}\cdot\mathrm{\Delta}1.0\mathrm{mM}$ , $50\mathrm{mL}$ ) solution was heated to $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a $100\mathrm{mL}$ Erlenmeyer flask. The sodium citrate solution $(1\%$ , 10 mL) was then added drop by drop and heated continuously for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . The AuNPs were successfully synthesized as the reaction solution changed from bright yellow to pink purple and stored for future usage. The synthesized AuNPs were examined by TEM (Figs. S2 and S3) and UV–vis spectro­ photometer while the highest absorption wavelength was located at 524 nm (Fig. S4).
## 2.5. Materials characterization
Surface morphologies were characterized by FE-SEM. Chemical compositions were examined by EDS. The crystal morphologies of the samples were measured by XRD and Raman microscope (Renishaw RM3000, Germany) with a 533 laser source. The enzyme in CA/ZIF8@enzyme was located by Confocal Laser Scanning Microscopy (CLSM, TCS SP8, Germany), while enzyme was labeled by FITC with excitation at $488~\mathrm{nm}$ and the fluorescence signal was collected in a window from 495 to $545\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S5). Detailed characterization is provided in the supporting material.
## 2.6. Electrochemical measurement
Herein, we established a simple EBFC-based self-powered biosensor to monitor glucose concentration (Scheme 1) and the photo image of asproposed self-powered biosensor is shown in Fig. S6. A Nafion $(5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ membrane was used to separate the dual-chamber biosensor which was made by two reagent bottles. As-prepared CA/ZIF-8@GOx/MWCNTs/ Au and CA/ZIF-8@LAC/MWCNTs/Au were used as anode and cathode, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology analysis
The morphologies of CA, CA/ZIF-8@enzyme, CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/ MWCNTs and CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au are shown in Fig. 1. Asreceived CA nanofiber membrane (Fig. 1a) are found to have a threedimensional network structure and smooth surface, which provides a transmission path for oxygen. The inset image of Fig. 1a shows the average diameter of CA nanofiber is about $233~\mathrm{nm}$ . The SEM image shown in Fig. 1b confirms that CA/ZIF-8@enzyme nanofiber membrane is constituted by CA and ZIF-8 crystal. Besides, ZIF-8 crystal $(\approx1.2\upmu\mathrm{m})$ is threaded by crystalline CA nanofiber, leading to a unique string-beaded morphology with a diameter of $1.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . After combined with MWCNTs, the CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs nanofiber membrane (Fig. 1c) with high-quality porous structure reaches a diameter of about $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , forming a denser hierarchical porous structure that facilitates ion transportation and electrolyte penetration. Moreover, the SEM image of CA/ZIF$8@$ enzyme (Fig. S7) at a higher magnification $(\mathbf{\nabla}\times5.0\mathbf{\k})$ shows the clearly boundaries and corners while the boundaries and corners were covered by MWCNTs on CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs (Fig. S8) mem­ brane. Fig. 1d shows the familiar morphology with CA/ZIF-8 $@$ enzyme/ MWCNTs nanofiber membrane after being adsorbed with AuNPs, and the inset image reveals the good hydrophilicity while the static water contact angle of CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au is $48.0^{\circ}$ , which ben­ efits for electrolyte penetration. Furthermore, in order to investigate the distribution of ZIF-8 and AuNPs throughout the composite membrane, SEM-EDS analysis is presented in Fig. 2a, it can effortlessly reveal the successfully overlaid CA surface by ZIF-8 (Fig. S9), MWCNTs (Fig. S10) and AuNPs. The elements’ concentrations of C, O, N, Zn and Au on CA/ ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au membrane are verified at about 61.89, 5.82, 15.63, 14.33 and $2.33~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively (Fig. S11).
## 3.2. XRD, Raman and TG analysis
Fig. 2b shows the XRD patterns for acquiring the variations of crystal structure on each sample. There is no significant peak on CA nanofiber membrane, indicating that the CA polymer exhibits characteristic of amorphous. After self-assembled of ZIF- $\cdot8@$ enzyme on the CA nano­ fibers, several diffraction peaks are appeared at $7.8^{\circ}$ , $10.9^{\circ}$ , $13.1^{\circ}$ , $15.1^{\circ}$ , $16.8^{\circ}$ , $18.4^{\circ}$ , $24.8^{\circ}$ and $26.9^{\circ}$ on CA/ZIF-8@enzyme nanofiber mem­ brane, which are consistent with the ZIF-8 and CA/ZIF-8 peaks in pre­ vious report (Fig. S12) (Li et al., 2019). The XRD pattern of CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs shows the graphite (002) plane and (101) crystal plane at $26.6^{\circ}$ and $43.3^{\circ}$ , respectively (Li et al., 2020b). With covered by the AuNPs, the CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au composite membrane depicts several peaks centered at 2θ of $38.6^{\circ}$ , $44.7^{\circ}$ and $78.4^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the diffraction of (111), (200) and (311) crystal planes of AuNPs, respectively (Hu et al., 2006).
The Raman spectra shown in Fig. Fig. S13 confirm the embedding of MWCNTs and AuNPs on CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au composite membrane. As can be seen, the spectra show three leading characteristic peaks of carbon structures D peak, G peak and 2D peak. G peak is caused by the scattering of $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ bonded carbon atoms in the graphite cluster at $1580\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Tadepalli et al., 2018) while the scattering of the disordered graphite carbon cluster forms the D-peak, which is centered at 1350 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The third peak (2D peak) at $2701\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ benefits from the highest optical branched-phonon boundary near $\mathbf{k}$ point at the Brillouin zone boundary (Yang et al., 2020). These three peaks above are significantly enhanced after the adsorption with AuNPs on the surface. This is due to the significant enhancement effect of AuNPs on Raman spectra (Sun et al., 2019b).
TG analysis is a typical method to evaluate the thermal stability of composite nanofiber membrane, and the results are shown in Fig. S14. CA is degraded in three steps. From 30 to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the first step is caused by the evaporation of residual absorbed water, whereas the followed steps of $100{-}300^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and $300{-}550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ represent the degradation of cel­ lulose network typically. The final weight losses of CA, CA/ZIF$8@$ enzyme, CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs and CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/ MWCNTs/Au are $92\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , $75\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , $58\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ and $46~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. The result shows the gradual enhancement of thermal stability with the addition of ZIF- ${8\textcircled{a}}$ enzyme, MWCNTs and Au step by step on composite nanofiber membrane. This is due to their coating on the surface and the presence of hydrogen bonds can improve the thermal properties of cellulose chains (Pan et al., 2011).
## 3.3. BET, XPS and mechanical properties
The BET curves of CA, CA/ZIF-8@enzyme and CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/ MWCNTs/Au shown in Fig. 2c are typical H4 hysteresis loops, which reveal the mesoporous structures of composite membranes. The BET surface area of CA $(9.879\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is consistent with average level of the nanofiber while the CA/ZIF-8 $@$ enzyme exhibits ultra-high level of $163.730\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is attribute to the porous structure and high absorption capacity of ZIF-8 crystal. In the process of in addition with MWCNTs and AuNPs, the composite membranes were immersed into water solution for several hours, which leading the swelling of CA nanofiber. This phenomenon may cause the damage of ZIF-8 on CA nanofiber and clog of external pores on ZIF-8, resulting in a significant reduction in the BET surface area of the CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs $(102.325~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au (99.356 $\mathbf{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\big]$ ) membranes.
X-ray test analyzed the elementary composition and electronic structure of C, O, N, Zn and Au on CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au electrode, and the spectra are depicted in Fig. S16. In the spectra of the samples after in-situ growth with ZIF-8@enzyme (i.e. CA/ZIF$8@$ enzyme, CA/ZIF-8@enzym/MWCNTs and CA/ZIF- $8@$ enzyme/ MWCNTs/Au), N1s spectrum is significant centered at 398.3 eV (Figs. S16a and d). Whereas the $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ atoms show two peaks of $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ located at 1021.9 and 1044.9 eV (Fig. S16e), which confirms the successful self-assemble of ZIF- $\cdot8@$ enzyme on the nanofibers. Moreover, the fitted Au 4f peaks of CA/ZIF- $8@$ enzyme/MWCNTs/Au nanofiber membrane consist of Au $4\mathrm{f}_{\ 5/2}$ and Au $4\mathrm{f}_{\ 7/2}$ at 87.1 and 83.5 eV (Fig. S16f), and it is contributed by the surface adsorption of AuNPs.
## 3.4. FV, FTIR and CLSM analysis
Fig. 3b exhibits the FTIR spectra of obtained membranes. The pure CA depicts several absorptions at 1741, 1373 and $1226~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , corre­ sponding to the characteristic band of $\mathtt{C}{=}0$ , C–O and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ of acetyl group (CHCOO), respectively. Moreover, the bands located in 1038 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ is caused by hydroxyl ring of cellulose ether. After the in-situ growth of ZIF-8 crystal, several new bands appear on the CA/ZIF-8 spectrum. Specifically, the out-of-plane blending on 2-MIM depicts two new bands at 690 and $757\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and the characteristic bands of inplane bending on 2-MIM ring are centered between 845 and $1310\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Meanwhile, the aliphatic and aromatic C–H stretch of 2-MIM corre­ sponds to a new band at $2900~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The spectrum of CA/ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@\mathbf{GOx}$ exhibits two additional bands at 1644 and $1537~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is attrib­ uted to amide-I and amide-II groups of GOx molecules, respectively. Moreover, amido-I band on the spectrum of CA/ZIF-8@LAC that located in $1641~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the C–N and N–H on LAC molecules, in addition, another sharp peak centered at $3432~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is also caused by LAC of $0{\mathrm{-H}}$ stretching. Nevertheless, these two bands move to the higher wavenumbers while they are centered at $1645~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and 3327 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ on the pure LAC, which may be caused by the vibrations of amide in the immobilization process (Patil and Yadav, 2018).
In order to further explore the spatial distribution of enzyme on the CA/ZIF-8 nanofiber membranes, CLSM results are shown in Fig. 3c, d and Fig. S17. The two images show almost identical results to indicate that the enzyme is distributed throughout CA/ZIF-8@enzyme, not only encapsulated in ZIF-8 crystals, but also on the CA nanofiber membranes. Moreover, the inset images of Fig. 3c and d clearly show the homoge­ neous distribution of enzyme molecules throughout ZIF-8, verifying the feasibility of enzyme encapsulation strategy.
## 3.5. Electrochemical behavior
As shown in Fig. 4c, the electrochemical performance of anode ex­ hibits a pair of redox peaks ${\left(\sim0.30\mathrm{\v}\right.}$ and ${\sim}0.43\mathrm{v})$ caused by the redox reaction of the anode enzyme (GOx molecules). As expected, the same phenomenon with the biocathode is found while the scan rate is increased from 50 to $300\ \mathrm{mV/s}$ . Fig. 4d exhibits a similar linear rela­ tionship between the oxidation and reduction currents with the increased scan rate, and it presents a good correlation while the ${\tt R}^{2}$ of $\mathbf{i_{\mathrm{pa}}}$ and $\mathbf{i}_{\mathrm{pc}}$ are 0.991 and 0.997, respectively. The good coupling between GOx and anode is also demonstrated and the process is therefore surfacecontrolled by the CA/ZIF-8@GOx/MWCNTs/Au electrode.
## 3.6. Self-powered biosensor performance
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was applied to char­ acterize the resistance of electron in the process of BFC operation, the resulted impedance spectra are shown in Fig. 5a. The charge transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{ct})}$ of CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs electrodes shows remarkably the increase compared to CA/ZIF-8/MWCNTs composite membranes, which is caused by the successful immobilization of enzyme (LAC and GOx). As-fabricated electrodes with lower $\mathrm{\mathbf{R_{ct}}}$ exhibit a broad prospect in sensor application after being modified with AuNPs, and this is attributed to the excellent electrical conductivity of the AuNPs.
A self-powered sensor was established by coupling the CA/ZIF$8@$ enzyme/MWCNTs electrodes under the optimal experimental con­ ditions. As expected, the variation of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}^{\mathrm{{ocr}}}$ was closely related to glucose concentration. In the range of $1{-}20~\mathrm{mM}$ , $\operatorname{E}^{\mathrm{{ocp}}}$ gradually increased with the elevated substrate concentration, but the change was slight after 10 mM(Fig. 5b), and the photo image of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}^{\mathrm{ocp}}$ for as-proposed biosensor under $10~\mathrm{mM}$ glucose is shown in Fig. S18. Furthermore, the effective work of as-proposed self-powered glucose biosensor was proved while an excellent linear relationship between $\operatorname{E}^{\mathrm{{ocr}}}$ and glucose concentration was fitted (Fig. 5c) from 1 to $10~\mathrm{mM}$ . The linear equation is defined as $\mathtt{E}^{\mathrm{OCP}}=26.346\mathrm{c}+12.438$ $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.996)$ ) with an outstanding detection limit of $5.347~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ based on $s/\Nu=3$ . Additionally, Fig. 5d exhibits the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}^{\mathrm{ocp}}$ curves and polarization curves under the condition of $10\ \mathrm{mM}$ glucose anolyte.
Long-term stability is a critical bottleneck for self-powered biosensor, particularly for non-invasive biosensors, continuous detec­ tion of up to $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ in this work are rare. It is verified that the sensor is capable of producing the same level of $\operatorname{E}^{\mathrm{{ocp}}}$ signal with refreshed sub­ strate, which suggests that the sensitivity of the sensor is well preserved. This is mainly due to the adequate protection of MOF encapsulation enzyme strategy in the designed nanostructures. However, the reduction of $\mathtt{E}^{\mathrm{{ocr}}}$ with the extension of working time can still be observed in Fig. 5e, which is a natural phenomenon, mainly due to the decrease of glucose concentration in the local environment and the loss of enzyme activity immobilized on CA nanofiber in the long-term measurement. In addition, the structure of as-proposed electrode (Fig. S19) shows no obvious change after $15\mathrm{h}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
The exploration of reliable long-term and real-time sensors to monitor human physiological conditions is of great significance for the increasingly ageing population (Lin et al., 2019b). Herein, we success­ fully encapsulated enzyme into ZIF-8 in the process of in-situ growth ZIF-8 on electrospun CA nanofiber membranes, and the CA/ZIF-8@enzyme/MWCNTs/Au membranes were utilized as highly flexible electrodes for self-powered glucose biosensor. Meanwhile, the as-proposed glucose biosensor held excellent long-term stability in continues works of up to $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As-proposed biosensor came up with a very meaningful strategy for future population research on the corre­ lation between glucose concentration in human sweat and physiological state.
